# Git ignore file for SOTI AI Analyser

# Zip files
*.zip

# Backups and temporary data
Backup/
Cases/
*.log
*.tmp

# OS specific files
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini

# IDE specific files
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
*.swo

# Extension artifacts
Extension/lib/jszip.min.js
Extension/lib/tesseract.min.js

# Extension signing key — private half of the manifest "key" that fixes the
# extension ID. Needed only to publish a .crx under the same ID. NEVER commit.
*.pem
soti-extension-signing-key.pem

# The compiled Salesforce Knowledge corpus. NEVER COMMIT — this repository is public.
#
# "KB Articles.md" is ~1,958 compiled Knowledge articles, and the code that loads it says
# what they are: "the engineer's own permissioned articles, compiled on their device by
# their own sync". That is internal SOTI Support content, not the public Pulse and docs
# pages the other knowledge/*.md files hold, and it does not belong in a public repository.
#
# It is also how a real credential nearly got out. GitHub push protection blocked a commit
# carrying this file because one article had pasted a live-shaped Slack user OAuth token
# (xoxp-…) into its worked example. That is the general shape of the risk: a support KB
# accumulates whatever engineers pasted into it over years, so the corpus cannot be
# reviewed once and then declared clean for ever.
#
# Globbed across every extension folder, because the folder name carries the version and is
# renamed on every release ("Extension v8.4.0" today).
#
# THE EXTENSION STILL SHIPS IT. It is the seed corpus a fresh install loads before the
# engineer has synced their own — see SF_KB_BUNDLED_PATH in sidepanel.js. It travels in the
# distributed folder, not through git, so anyone rebuilding a release folder from this
# repository has to put it back in by hand or leave first-run search empty until a sync.
Extension*/knowledge/KB Articles.md
Extension*/knowledge/KBArticles.md

# THE QA TOOL IS ITS OWN REPOSITORY, and lives at "QA Tool v1.0.0/" inside this
# working tree. It has its own .git, so git will not recurse into it anyway —
# but without this rule `git add -A` here records it as a GITLINK: a one-line
# submodule entry pointing at a commit hash, with no submodule configured and no
# URL to fetch it from. Anybody cloning this repository then gets an empty
# directory and no explanation.
#
# Globbed on the version, because the folder name carries it and is renamed on
# every release — the same reason the Extension* rules below are globbed.
QA Tool*/
